WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.120 align:start position:19%
My dash cam recorded 4 hours of empty
highway that night and I have not slept

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:13.240 align:start position:19%
right since I watched it.
In every single frame, I am dead asleep

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:18.720 align:start position:19%
in the sleeper berth behind the cab,
mouth open, snoring.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:23.080 align:start position:19%
And in the driver seat, hands on my
wheel,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.080 align:start position:19%
there is someone else.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:31.520 align:start position:19%
I have watched that footage 41 times. It
has never once looked different.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:36.520 align:start position:19%
My name is Dale Mercer. I drive night
freight for Halverson Logistics.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.880 align:start position:19%
3 years ago, my rig got broken into at a
rest stop outside Camden while I was

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.760 align:start position:19%
inside buying coffee.
They took a pallet of electronics and my

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.000 align:start position:19%
favorite jacket.
After that, the company made us install

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.520 align:start position:19%
dash cams, front cab, sleeper, and
cargo.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.760 align:start position:19%
Three cameras recording every single
second of every run. Back then, I

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.960 align:start position:19%
thought it was a good idea. Protection,
you know?

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.280 align:start position:19%
October the 9th, I picked up a load of
paper towels in Trenton bound for

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:07.200 align:start position:19%
Atlantic City.
Dispatch offered me the turnpike tolls

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.160 align:start position:19%
and all. I took the shortcut instead
because I am cheap and I was running

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.280 align:start position:19%
late.
County Route 532, straight through the

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:19.320 align:start position:19%
Pine Barrens. 40 miles of black pines
and nothing else.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:25.280 align:start position:19%
The Barrens have stories.
The Jersey Devil, the ghost lights,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:30.400 align:start position:19%
everybody in Jersey knows them.
I did not believe any of it. I was wrong

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:37.080 align:start position:19%
about that.
Mile marker 41, just after midnight, my

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:41.440 align:start position:19%
headlights caught him on the shoulder.
A man in a gray hoodie, soaked through,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.960 align:start position:19%
thumb out, standing perfectly still in
the rain, like a statue somebody left

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.000 align:start position:19%
there.
Company policy is clear. We do not stop

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:53.360 align:start position:19%
for hitchhikers. Ever.
I kept driving in the mirror, he did not

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.000 align:start position:19%
move a muscle.
He just stood there and watched me pass.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:01.680 align:start position:19%
Head turned,
Tracking me.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.880 align:start position:19%
7 mi later at mile marker 48, my
headlights caught him again.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:11.560 align:start position:19%
Same gray hoodie, same thumb out, same
stillness.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:17.680 align:start position:19%
That is impossible. Nobody walks 7 mi in
4 minutes. I did the math twice.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.480 align:start position:19%
I told myself it was a different guy.
A coincidence.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:26.280 align:start position:19%
The Barrens do strange things to tired
eyes at 1:00 in the morning.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.520 align:start position:19%
At mile marker 55, he was standing in
the middle of my lane.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.520 align:start position:19%
I slammed the brakes and the trailer
jackknifed. And when the headlights

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:40.800 align:start position:19%
steadied, the road was empty.
My heart was hammering so hard the dash

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.800 align:start position:19%
cam audio picked it up.
I got out with my flashlight, hand

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:49.320 align:start position:19%
shaking so bad the beam was jumping
everywhere. Checked the front bumper,

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.880 align:start position:19%
checked the grill.
Nothing. No blood, no dent, not even a

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.440 align:start position:19%
footprint in the mud.
That is when I noticed the sleeper

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.959 align:start position:19%
curtain was swaying.
I never sleep with that curtain open.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.120 align:start position:19%
Always close it before I roll.
I told myself the hard braking threw it

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.959 align:start position:19%
loose.
I told myself a lot of things that

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.640 align:start position:19%
night, and I believed almost none of
them.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:17.320 align:start position:19%
I climbed back in, locked the doors, and
drove.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.920 align:start position:19%
The CB had been quiet all night. Then it
crackled.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:28.000 align:start position:19%
A voice said very clearly, in my own
voice, "Dale.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:32.920 align:start position:19%
Do not look back."
I nearly put the rig in the ditch.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.760 align:start position:19%
Here's the thing about fear.
It makes you run a checklist just to

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:43.120 align:start position:19%
feel sane.
Doors locked, windows up, sleeper empty.

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:47.959 align:start position:19%
I checked with a flashlight.
Empty. Of course it was empty.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:52.239 align:start position:19%
This one genuinely scared me when I
watched it back later because the dash

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:57.720 align:start position:19%
cam caught what I missed.
On the sleeper camera at the exact

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:01.880 align:start position:19%
second the CB spoke, the curtain moved
by itself.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.040 align:start position:19%
I drove the last 20 miles with the cab
light burning, hunched over the wheel

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:10.040 align:start position:19%
like a scared kid who thinks the dark is
full of teeth.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:13.640 align:start position:19%
I made the delivery in Atlantic City at
4:00 in the morning.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:18.000 align:start position:19%
The dock guy asked why I looked like I
had seen a ghost.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:22.040 align:start position:19%
I almost told him. Then I saw the
trailer seal was broke.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:27.000 align:start position:19%
The back doors were standing open. The
load was fine, but the doors were open.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.200 align:start position:19%
I never open those doors. The seal was
cut clean,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:34.640 align:start position:19%
like with a knife.
Inside the trailer, in the thin dust on

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.960 align:start position:19%
the floor lit by my flashlight, there
were footprints.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.960 align:start position:19%
Fresh ones.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:46.800 align:start position:19%
Muddy boot prints walking from the doors
to the very back wall and stopping

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.840 align:start position:19%
there. No prints coming back.
The dock guy went pale. He said, "Man,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:54.320 align:start position:19%
you picked up a rider."
I did

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.160 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Yunobi. Welcome to Tales of
Dread.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.880 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; He did not answer. I was already
thinking about the footage.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:04.760 align:start position:19%
Back at the motel, hands still shaking,
I pulled the memory card, plugged it

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:10.320 align:start position:19%
into my laptop, and watched all three
cameras straight through.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:14.840 align:start position:19%
Front cab, just me, white knuckled,
talking to nobody.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:18.680 align:start position:19%
Sleeper, the curtain moving at the exact
second the CB spoke.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:23.960 align:start position:19%
Nothing else.
Cargo, the doors swinging open by

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:29.040 align:start position:19%
themselves at 1:47 a.m. The footprints
appearing one by one.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:34.960 align:start position:19%
And then I saw it in the cargo camera,
behind the stacked pallet, for exactly

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:40.000 align:start position:19%
three frames, half a second of video.
The gray hoodie, a face that was just

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:44.919 align:start position:19%
wrong, too long, smiling wrong.
I called my dispatcher and told him I

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.400 align:start position:19%
was done with night runs.
He laughed. Then he watched the clip I

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.520 align:start position:19%
sent him.
Then he stopped laughing.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:59.640 align:start position:19%
That night at the motel, I dreamed the
CB voice said my name.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:03.680 align:start position:19%
And I woke up with the TV on, tuned to
static.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.320 align:start position:19%
I still think about mile marker 41 every
time I pass a hitchhiker I do not pick

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.960 align:start position:19%
up.
Which is every hitchhiker's now and

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.720 align:start position:19%
forever.
Two weeks later the company sent me the

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.360 align:start position:19%
archive footage.
Routine backup.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.240 align:start position:19%
I almost deleted the whole folder
without watching a second of it. God, I

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.000 align:start position:19%
wish I had.
There was a fourth camera file, one I

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:31.080 align:start position:19%
never installed.
Timestamped 3:33 a.m., labeled rear

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:37.320 align:start position:19%
view.
My dash cam does not have a rear view

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.320 align:start position:19%
camera. My truck does not have one.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:45.720 align:start position:19%
The clip shows the sleeper berth. I am
asleep, and in the driver seat, hands on

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:49.000 align:start position:19%
my wheel, the gray hoodie is driving my
truck.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.800 align:start position:19%
At the very end of the clip, the last 2
seconds, it turns its head toward the

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.880 align:start position:19%
camera.
Slowly, like it knew I would be

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:59.640 align:start position:19%
watching.
The face is too long, the smile is

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:04.120 align:start position:19%
wrong, and it says, in my voice, "Thanks
for the ride, Dale."

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:09.040 align:start position:19%
I am not saying you should believe me. I
barely believe me.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:13.320 align:start position:19%
I am just saying I do not drive nights
anymore, and I sleep with every light in

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:17.480 align:start position:19%
the house on.
If this one got under your skin,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:22.800 align:start position:19%
subscribe to Midnight Dread Tales, a new
horror story every single night.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:29.400 align:start position:19%
And if you ever drive 532 after
midnight, do not stop. Whatever you do,

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.400 align:start position:19%
do not stop.

